WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.967 --> 00:00:02.735
[music playing]

00:00:02.802 --> 00:00:03.903
 My name is Justin Workman.

00:00:03.970 --> 00:00:05.171
I'm 39.

00:00:05.238 --> 00:00:07.506 line:0%
And I've been bladesmithing
for roughly four years.

00:00:07.573 --> 00:00:09.542
The last three months, I've
been going through chemo

00:00:09.609 --> 00:00:11.678
with the hopes that it
would stave off the need

00:00:11.744 --> 00:00:13.379
for amputation of my right arm.

00:00:13.445 --> 00:00:17.149
I hope this sets an example
to anybody that's struggling.

00:00:17.216 --> 00:00:22.521
No matter how bad it is, you
can still power through it.

00:00:22.588 --> 00:00:23.389
 My name is Doug Zegel.

00:00:23.456 --> 00:00:24.957
I'm from Mount
Laurel, New Jersey.

00:00:24.990 --> 00:00:26.993 line:7%
I'm 33 years old.

00:00:27.060 --> 00:00:28.427
My uncle was a blacksmith.

00:00:28.494 --> 00:00:31.698
So I got to move some metal
with him when I was younger.

00:00:31.731 --> 00:00:33.133
And I really enjoyed it.

00:00:33.199 --> 00:00:35.969
You took something rock hard,
like steel, you heat it up,

00:00:36.002 --> 00:00:37.370
and it's like Play-Doh.

00:00:37.436 --> 00:00:40.106
I mean, what's cooler than that?

00:00:40.173 --> 00:00:41.507
GRADY POWELL: Justin,
Doug, congratulations.

00:00:41.540 --> 00:00:43.509
You guys made it past
round one and two.

00:00:43.576 --> 00:00:45.611
And now it's just
between the two of you

00:00:45.678 --> 00:00:47.146
for the title of "Forged
in Fire" Champion

00:00:47.213 --> 00:00:48.848
and a check for $10,000.

00:00:48.882 --> 00:00:50.283
In round three of
the competition,

00:00:50.349 --> 00:00:52.118
we're going to send you home
to make another culinary tool.

00:00:52.185 --> 00:00:53.586
But this one is
quite a bit bigger

00:00:53.620 --> 00:00:54.520
than anything you've seen.

00:00:54.554 --> 00:00:59.292
And that tool is
the maguro bocho.

00:01:01.527 --> 00:01:03.062
NARRATOR: Also known
as the tuna sword,

00:01:03.129 --> 00:01:04.697
the maguro bocho
is a specialized

00:01:04.764 --> 00:01:07.667
cutting tool designed to
fillet massive bluefin tuna.

00:01:07.700 --> 00:01:10.503
This impressive blade is not
your average kitchen cutlery.

00:01:10.569 --> 00:01:12.472
With a total length
up to three feet long,

00:01:12.538 --> 00:01:14.473
these culinary swords
were often hand

00:01:14.540 --> 00:01:16.609
forged by the same
swordsmiths that made

00:01:16.676 --> 00:01:18.144
the iconic Japanese katana.

00:01:18.210 --> 00:01:20.146
They feature a thick
spine and razor-sharp

00:01:20.213 --> 00:01:22.949
edge that cuts effortlessly
through any large fish.

00:01:22.982 --> 00:01:26.619
The maguro bocho is still used
today by some Japanese chefs

00:01:26.686 --> 00:01:29.022
and can be seen on
full display at some

00:01:29.088 --> 00:01:32.258
of Tokyo's iconic fish markets.

00:01:32.325 --> 00:01:33.492
 Now, when you guys
build your blades,

00:01:33.559 --> 00:01:35.161
I want you to follow
these parameters.

00:01:35.195 --> 00:01:38.030
Your blade length needs to
be between 29 and 31 inches.

00:01:38.097 --> 00:01:40.433
You need to have a
single-edge chisel grind.

00:01:40.500 --> 00:01:41.968
And I also want
you to incorporate

00:01:42.035 --> 00:01:44.637
a ricasso between the
handle and the blade.

00:01:44.704 --> 00:01:45.905
 I'm feeling pretty nervous.

00:01:45.972 --> 00:01:49.141
I'm going to have to forge the
longest blade I've ever made.

00:01:49.175 --> 00:01:50.810
 Gentlemen, we'll see
you guys in four days.

00:01:50.877 --> 00:01:52.044
Good luck.
- All right.

00:01:52.111 --> 00:01:53.113
 Good luck, man.

00:01:57.883 --> 00:02:01.053
 We're here at my home forge
in Chambersburg, Pennsylvania.

00:02:01.087 --> 00:02:04.023
My game plan is, I'm
going to san mai.

00:02:04.090 --> 00:02:05.391 line:7%
Give her a little flux.

00:02:05.458 --> 00:02:07.093 line:0%
JUSTIN WORKMAN (VOICEOVER):
heat treat is the biggest

00:02:07.160 --> 00:02:08.261
thing I'm concerned with.

00:02:08.327 --> 00:02:10.663
My goal is to get
a hardened blade.

00:02:13.766 --> 00:02:18.104
 Oh, I got a nice little
warp in that bad boy.

00:02:18.171 --> 00:02:19.438
Oh, I'm so mad.

00:02:19.505 --> 00:02:20.940
JUSTIN WORKMAN (VOICEOVER):
whole competition did

00:02:20.973 --> 00:02:22.775
not go exactly as I planned.

00:02:22.842 --> 00:02:25.478
Got about four hours
left of this competition,

00:02:25.545 --> 00:02:28.348
and I still have a fair
amount of work to do.

00:02:28.381 --> 00:02:30.349
 I know for a fact
that I met parameters.

00:02:30.416 --> 00:02:32.785
It's the one thing
that I've been checking

00:02:32.851 --> 00:02:35.155
over, and over, and over again.

00:02:38.390 --> 00:02:40.159
DOUG ZEGEL: I'm back in
Mount Laurel, New Jersey,

00:02:40.225 --> 00:02:41.560
at my home forge.

00:02:41.627 --> 00:02:43.896
And I am ready to get started
on my maguro bocho build.

00:02:43.963 --> 00:02:46.966
So this tuna sword is
probably about twice as long

00:02:46.999 --> 00:02:48.801
as anything I've ever made.

00:02:48.868 --> 00:02:50.270
I gotta get the forge hot.

00:02:50.336 --> 00:02:55.240
And I'm ready to put my 80CRV2
in there and get hammering.

00:02:55.307 --> 00:02:56.542
It's pretty malleable steel.

00:02:56.609 --> 00:02:59.378
So, so far things are
going pretty quickly.

00:02:59.445 --> 00:03:02.114
But I want to keep making
progress as fast as possible.

00:03:02.181 --> 00:03:03.383
Once the quench
comes, that's kind

00:03:03.449 --> 00:03:05.551
of the make-or-break
situation for me today.

00:03:05.617 --> 00:03:07.554
So I'm just trying to
get the heat right.

00:03:11.256 --> 00:03:13.225
Yeah, we're in good shape.

00:03:13.292 --> 00:03:15.294
It's time to get everything
fit and assembled

00:03:15.361 --> 00:03:17.563
and finish this blade.

00:03:17.630 --> 00:03:18.264
All right.

00:03:18.297 --> 00:03:19.732
Got a nice, clean fit.

00:03:19.799 --> 00:03:22.301
That is the first look at what
will soon be a finished blade.

00:03:22.368 --> 00:03:24.404
Now that I have the
final assembly finished--

00:03:24.437 --> 00:03:26.439
I didn't come up with
an elaborate test.

00:03:26.505 --> 00:03:29.007
But I used to work in
a sushi restaurant,

00:03:29.041 --> 00:03:32.078
and this is a tuna sword.

00:03:32.145 --> 00:03:35.381
It cut pretty clean.

00:03:35.448 --> 00:03:38.050
I think it does the job.

00:03:38.117 --> 00:03:39.419
 All right, bladesmiths.

00:03:39.485 --> 00:03:40.887
To find out what
kind of lethal damage

00:03:40.954 --> 00:03:43.689
your magura bochos will
do, I'll take your weapon

00:03:43.756 --> 00:03:49.194
and make some lethal cuts on
these six different fishes.

00:03:49.261 --> 00:03:50.029
Justin, you're up first.

00:03:50.096 --> 00:03:50.929
You ready for this?

00:03:50.996 --> 00:03:53.165
 Let's make some sushi.

00:03:53.232 --> 00:03:56.836
[heavy metal music]

00:04:07.346 --> 00:04:08.514
 Way to go, man.

00:04:08.547 --> 00:04:09.315
 Thanks.

00:04:09.382 --> 00:04:10.449
DOUG MARCAIDA:
All right, Justin.

00:04:10.516 --> 00:04:12.318
Let's talk about
your weapon here.

00:04:12.351 --> 00:04:14.420
First up, your edge--

00:04:14.487 --> 00:04:16.755
one-and-done for
all the cuts here.

00:04:16.822 --> 00:04:19.092
It's a very sharp edge.

00:04:19.158 --> 00:04:20.693
I like the handle
construction here.

00:04:20.759 --> 00:04:24.597
It's ovoid, so my hand matches
nicely and wraps around it.

00:04:24.630 --> 00:04:27.533
Overall, sir, it will kill.

00:04:27.600 --> 00:04:29.068
 Thank you.

00:04:29.102 --> 00:04:30.636
 All right, Doug,
you're up next.

00:04:30.703 --> 00:04:31.203
You ready?

00:04:31.270 --> 00:04:32.071
 Have at it.

00:04:32.104 --> 00:04:33.339
Let's do it.

00:04:33.406 --> 00:04:37.543
[heavy metal music]

00:04:48.087 --> 00:04:49.688
 Nicely job, man.

00:04:49.722 --> 00:04:50.289
 It's all right.

00:04:50.355 --> 00:04:51.390
 All right, Doug.

00:04:51.457 --> 00:04:53.426
First up, the handle
construction--

00:04:53.493 --> 00:04:55.360
I really like the way you
shaped your handle here.

00:04:55.427 --> 00:04:57.196
It's typical Japanese style.

00:04:57.263 --> 00:04:59.565
Now, it's a lighter blade.

00:04:59.632 --> 00:05:04.670
But your edge is so sharp, it
cut cleanly through the fishes

00:05:04.737 --> 00:05:05.504
easily.

00:05:05.537 --> 00:05:08.574
Overall, sir, it will kill.

00:05:08.607 --> 00:05:09.309
 Thanks.

00:05:11.477 --> 00:05:12.544
DAVID BAKER: All
right, gentlemen.

00:05:12.611 --> 00:05:13.613
I think you know
what time it is.

00:05:13.679 --> 00:05:14.914
It's time for the strength test.

00:05:14.980 --> 00:05:18.117
And we're calling this
our bo staff attack.

00:05:18.184 --> 00:05:21.120
Now, Justin, your weapon is
already locked in our device

00:05:21.187 --> 00:05:24.690
here, and I will be attacking
it with our wax wood bo staffs.

00:05:24.756 --> 00:05:27.459
This test is not about what
your blades do to that staff,

00:05:27.526 --> 00:05:29.461
but what that staff's
gonna do to your blade.

00:05:29.528 --> 00:05:30.996
JUSTIN WORKMAN: All right.
- Y'all ready?

00:05:31.030 --> 00:05:31.997
 Let's do it.

00:05:32.031 --> 00:05:32.732
 OK.

00:05:37.903 --> 00:05:41.474
[heavy metal music]

00:05:55.788 --> 00:05:56.955
 All right, Justin.

00:05:56.989 --> 00:05:59.559
You know, this is a brutal
test for what is technically

00:05:59.592 --> 00:06:01.159
a large kitchen knife.

00:06:01.226 --> 00:06:03.562
But you did very, very well.

00:06:03.629 --> 00:06:07.700
Right where my finger is,
there's the smallest of chips.

00:06:07.766 --> 00:06:09.869
And it's-- when I say
"smallest of chips,"

00:06:09.936 --> 00:06:11.637
I mean, it's really small.

00:06:11.703 --> 00:06:14.173
Everything else looks
right and tight.

00:06:14.239 --> 00:06:15.808
All in all, you did a great job.

00:06:15.875 --> 00:06:16.408
You survived.

00:06:16.475 --> 00:06:17.276
 Thank you.

00:06:17.343 --> 00:06:18.443
DAVID BAKER: You bet.

00:06:18.510 --> 00:06:19.845
All right, Doug.
You're up.

00:06:19.912 --> 00:06:20.479
You ready?

00:06:20.546 --> 00:06:21.680
 As ready as I can be.

00:06:21.747 --> 00:06:24.116
 OK, because this
is fun for me.

00:06:24.183 --> 00:06:27.520
DOUG ZEGEL: These blades are
narrow, really thin blades.

00:06:27.586 --> 00:06:30.022
It is not designed
to take any abuse.

00:06:30.089 --> 00:06:34.527
I'm already thinking that
Justin's got this test won.

00:06:34.594 --> 00:06:38.131
[heavy metal music]

00:06:49.008 --> 00:06:53.378
 So Doug, right off,
this is remarkably light.

00:06:53.445 --> 00:06:55.814
There is the
slightest rough spot.

00:06:55.881 --> 00:06:59.885
But besides that, I don't see
any deformation in your blade.

00:06:59.919 --> 00:07:03.488
Blade's as straight as it
was when we started out.

00:07:03.522 --> 00:07:04.857
It's a good, solid piece.

00:07:04.924 --> 00:07:05.424
Well done.

00:07:05.491 --> 00:07:06.559
 Thank you.

00:07:06.626 --> 00:07:07.726
DAVID BAKER: So all
right, gentlemen.

00:07:07.793 --> 00:07:09.929
Two tests down and
one test to go.

00:07:12.898 --> 00:07:14.867
DOUG MARCAIDA: All
right, bladesmiths,

00:07:14.934 --> 00:07:16.301
we're cutting it close.

00:07:16.368 --> 00:07:18.637
We know that both
your weapons can kill

00:07:18.704 --> 00:07:20.005
and we know they're strong.

00:07:20.039 --> 00:07:22.508
Now's the time to find
out how sharp they are.

00:07:22.541 --> 00:07:25.377
This is the sharpness test.

00:07:25.444 --> 00:07:27.313
To find out how sharp
your weapons are,

00:07:27.380 --> 00:07:29.681
I will cut through these
rolled-up tatami mats.

00:07:29.748 --> 00:07:31.384
Now, unlike the
strength test, this

00:07:31.450 --> 00:07:33.886
is all about what the
edge of your weapon

00:07:33.953 --> 00:07:35.988
will do to these mats.

00:07:36.021 --> 00:07:36.622
Justin, you're up first.

00:07:36.689 --> 00:07:37.389
You ready for this?

00:07:37.456 --> 00:07:38.257
 Rock and roll.

00:07:38.324 --> 00:07:39.192
 All right, do that.

00:07:43.562 --> 00:07:46.799
[heavy metal music]

00:07:56.442 --> 00:07:58.677
All right Justin, let's
talk about the cuts here.

00:07:58.710 --> 00:08:02.248
Clearly, on the other cuts,
they cut all the way through.

00:08:02.314 --> 00:08:06.252
On the last cut, almost, but
just a little skin of that mat

00:08:06.319 --> 00:08:07.452
is hanging on.

00:08:07.519 --> 00:08:09.388
But it is a sharp edge.

00:08:09.455 --> 00:08:11.823
Overall, sir, it will cut.

00:08:11.890 --> 00:08:12.691
 Thank you.

00:08:12.758 --> 00:08:14.026
 All right, Doug, your turn.

00:08:14.060 --> 00:08:14.827
So you ready?

00:08:14.894 --> 00:08:16.028
 Let's see what
this thing can do.

00:08:16.095 --> 00:08:16.829
 Let's do it.

00:08:24.569 --> 00:08:27.707
[heavy metal music]

00:08:40.452 --> 00:08:41.587
All right, Doug.

00:08:41.620 --> 00:08:44.423
First cut just went
so easy without a drag

00:08:44.490 --> 00:08:46.625
that it went into
the next target.

00:08:46.692 --> 00:08:48.093
It is a very sharp weapon.

00:08:48.160 --> 00:08:49.461
It's very easy to wield.

00:08:49.528 --> 00:08:51.430
And it's light.

00:08:51.497 --> 00:08:54.800
Overall, sir, it will cut.

00:08:54.867 --> 00:08:55.801
 Thank you.

00:08:55.868 --> 00:08:57.069
GRADY POWELL: First
of all, both of you,

00:08:57.136 --> 00:08:58.437
thank you very much
for your hard work.

00:08:58.504 --> 00:08:59.871
But as we know,
only one of you is

00:08:59.905 --> 00:09:02.174
going to be leaving here the
"Forged in Fire" Champion

00:09:02.241 --> 00:09:03.975
with a check for $10,000.

00:09:04.009 --> 00:09:09.214
And today's "Forged in
Fire" champion is Doug.

00:09:09.248 --> 00:09:10.483
Congratulations, you won.

00:09:10.549 --> 00:09:12.384
Justin, unfortunately,
you're not gonna

00:09:12.450 --> 00:09:13.485
be leaving with the win today.

00:09:13.552 --> 00:09:14.753
And Doug is going
to tell you why.

00:09:14.820 --> 00:09:17.055
 Justin, on any other
given competition,

00:09:17.122 --> 00:09:19.257
I can see your blade
being a champion's blade.

00:09:19.324 --> 00:09:20.726
It is a beautiful blade.

00:09:20.760 --> 00:09:23.529
But today, your opponent's was
just a little sharper, a little

00:09:23.595 --> 00:09:25.130
lighter, and faster to wield.

00:09:25.197 --> 00:09:27.099
 Justin, again, thank you
very much for your hard work.

00:09:27.166 --> 00:09:28.601
But unfortunately,
I'm gonna have to ask

00:09:28.667 --> 00:09:29.935
you to please leave the forge.

00:09:30.002 --> 00:09:31.036
 Good job.

00:09:31.103 --> 00:09:32.271
 Thank you.

00:09:32.338 --> 00:09:36.275
 I'm glad that this time
frame worked out as it did.

00:09:36.341 --> 00:09:38.010
Chemo takes a toll on your body.

00:09:38.077 --> 00:09:40.312
But going through
that and then do

00:09:40.379 --> 00:09:44.649
this whole competition just
goes to show myself how much

00:09:44.716 --> 00:09:47.253
I can actually push through.

00:09:47.320 --> 00:09:48.554
I've always kind
of been stubborn.

00:09:48.621 --> 00:09:53.626
But I never realized how
stubborn I am, actually.

00:09:53.693 --> 00:09:57.229
 Well, Doug, you are the
"Forged in Fire" Champion.

00:09:57.296 --> 00:09:58.364
Congratulations, man.

00:09:58.431 --> 00:09:59.097
 Thanks a lot.

00:09:59.164 --> 00:10:00.098
[applause]

00:10:00.165 --> 00:10:01.600
 What are you gonna to
get with that $10,000?

00:10:01.667 --> 00:10:04.236
 Oh, I fell in love
with a forge press.

00:10:04.303 --> 00:10:05.204
[laughs]

00:10:05.237 --> 00:10:07.673
I definitely want one
of those of my own.

00:10:07.707 --> 00:10:08.807
I'm ecstatic.

00:10:08.874 --> 00:10:10.409
You know, I put a
lot of heart and soul

00:10:10.442 --> 00:10:11.644
into making that blade.

00:10:11.710 --> 00:10:13.446
And seeing it perform
the way that it did

00:10:13.512 --> 00:10:15.647
was just a really
great confirmation

00:10:15.714 --> 00:10:16.949
about my abilities.

00:10:16.983 --> 00:10:18.684
And it's just making me
want to make more knives.

00:10:18.751 --> 00:10:22.688
[heavy metal music]

